<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">60 or 61 CE · Roman Britain</p><h1 class="sp-title">Boudica before the Final Battle</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>When Prasutagus, king of the Iceni in eastern Britain, died, Roman officials seized his kingdom, flogged his widow Boudica and raped their daughters. Boudica led the Iceni and their neighbours in a revolt that destroyed Camulodunum, Londinium and Verulamium. As the governor Suetonius Paulinus brought her army to battle, Tacitus has her ride along the ranks in a chariot with her daughters.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>This is a woman's resolve; as for men, they may live and be slaves.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Tacitus, Annals, XIV.35, tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb (1876). Tacitus wrote some fifty years later, and the words are his.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Annals_(Tacitus)/Book_14#35" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource, tr. Church and Brodribb</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The Romans, though heavily outnumbered, won a crushing victory at an unknown site, probably in the Midlands, and Tacitus reports that Boudica took poison. Rome kept its province, but Paulinus was soon replaced by a more conciliatory governor. Remembered again once Tacitus was rediscovered in the Renaissance, Boudica became a heroine of British national legend.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-conquest-of-britain">Roman conquest of Britain</a></div></div></section>
